{"name":"Nature Spirits","hex":"#c8c8b4","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"nature","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The way things are, the totality of all things in the physical universe and their order, especially the physical world in contrast to spiritual realms and flora and fauna as distinct from human conventions, art, and technology.","The particular way someone or something is, especially\nThe essential or innate characteristics of a person or thing which will always tend to manifest, especially in contrast to specific contexts, reason, religious duty, upbringing, and personal pretense or effort."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To endow with natural qualities."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/nature"},{"term":"spirits","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["third-person singular simple present indicative of spirit"]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of spirit"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/spirits"}],"entries":[{"title":"List of nature deities","blocks":[{"text":"In religion, a nature deity is a deity in charge of forces of nature, such as water, biological processes, or weather. These deities can also govern natural features such as mountains, trees, or volcanoes. Accepted in animism, pantheism, panentheism, polytheism, deism, totemism, shamanism, Taoism, Hinduism, and paganism, the nature deity can embody a number of archetypes including mother goddess, Mother Nature, or lord of the animals."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/Evelyn_De_Morgan_-_The_Dryad.jpg/330px-Evelyn_De_Morgan_-_The_Dryad.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_nature_deities","wikidata":"Q6630292"},{"title":"Animism","blocks":[{"text":"Animism is the worldview or perception that various types of natural phenomena, including non-human organisms and often even landforms and inanimate objects, possess distinct spiritual essences, souls, or personhood. Animism assumes a wide view about which entities may be considered animate, thus typically perceiving animals, plants, rivers, weather systems, and other natural entities, plus in some cases human handiwork and words, as all having some degree of agency, life, and will. Animism is used in anthropology of religion as a term for the belief system of many indigenous peoples in contrast to the relatively more recent development of organized religions."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Animism","wikidata":"Q43004"},{"title":"Nature","blocks":[{"text":"Nature, in the broadest sense, is the physical world or universe. In this general sense nature refers to the laws, elements and phenomena of the physical world, including life. Although humans are part of nature, human activity or humans as a whole are often described as at times at odds, or outright separate and even superior to nature."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Startrails_above_Gunung_Bromo_-_Indonesia.jpg/330px-Startrails_above_Gunung_Bromo_-_Indonesia.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nature","wikidata":"Q7860"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Edward_Burnett_Tylor.jpg/250px-Edward_Burnett_Tylor.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/12/Edward_Burnett_Tylor.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Edward_Burnett_Tylor.jpg/250px-Edward_Burnett_Tylor.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Edward Tylor developed animism as an anthropological theory.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEdward_Burnett_Tylor.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.